✅ ArrayList vs LinkedList in Java

| Feature                     | `ArrayList`                                      | `LinkedList`                                              |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| Structure                   | Resizable array                                  | Doubly linked list (nodes)                                |
| Access `get(i)`             | ✅ `O(1)` — direct index                          | ❌ `O(n)` — traverse nodes                                |
| Insert/delete at **end**    | ✅ Amortized `O(1)`                               | ✅ `O(1)`                                                 |
| Insert/delete at **start**  | ❌ `O(n)` — shift elements                        | ✅ `O(1)`                                                 |
| Insert/delete in **middle** | ❌ `O(n)` — shift elements                        | ❌ Still `O(n)` to reach index; link/unlink itself is `O(1)` |
| Memory                      | Less per element (array of refs; may have unused capacity) | More (each node: data + prev + next)               |
| Iteration                   | ✅ Faster (contiguous / cache-friendly)           | ❌ Slower (scattered nodes)                               |
| Random access               | ✅ Efficient (`RandomAccess`)                     | ❌ Not efficient                                          |
| Implements                  | `List`, `RandomAccess`                           | `List`, `Deque`, `Queue`                                  |
| Thread-safe?                | ❌ No                                            | ❌ No                                                     |
| Best for                    | Fast reads / index access                        | Frequent add/remove at ends; queue/deque/stack use        |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔧 Internal working of ArrayList

ArrayList is backed by a regular `Object[]` array:

```java
transient Object[] elementData; // backing array
private int size;               // number of elements actually stored
```

| Concept    | Meaning |
| ---------- | ------- |
| `size`     | How many elements are in the list |
| `capacity` | Length of the backing array (`elementData.length`) — can be > `size` |

How main operations work:
1) **`get(i)`** → return `elementData[i]` → `O(1)`
2) **`add(e)` at end**
   - If `size == capacity` → grow the array (usually to ~1.5× old capacity)
   - Then put element at `elementData[size]` and increment `size`
   - Amortized `O(1)`
3) **`add(i, e)` / `remove(i)` in middle**
   - Shift elements with `System.arraycopy` (right for insert, left for remove)
   - `O(n)` because of shifting
4) **Growth** (when full)
   - Create a larger array
   - Copy old elements into it
   - Continue the insert

```text
capacity = 10, size = 3
[ A | B | C | _ | _ | _ | _ | _ | _ | _ ]
              ↑
            size
```

Notes interviewers like:
- Empty `new ArrayList<>()` starts with capacity **0**; first `add` grows it (commonly to **10**).
- Extra unused slots = capacity − size (memory overhead vs LinkedList’s per-node pointers).
- Not synchronized; iterators are fail-fast (`ConcurrentModificationException` if structurally modified during iteration).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔍 Code example

```java
List<String> arrayList = new ArrayList<>();
List<String> linkedList = new LinkedList<>();

arrayList.add("A");
linkedList.add("A");
```

Same `List` API from the outside — difference is internal structure and performance.

⚠️ `linkedList.get(i)` is `O(n)` because it walks from the head (or tail) to that index.

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 When to use what

✅ Use **ArrayList** if:
- You need fast random access (`get(i)`)
- Workload is mostly reads / iteration
- Default choice for most list use cases

✅ Use **LinkedList** if:
- Lots of insert/delete at the **beginning or ends**
- You need queue / deque / stack behavior (`addFirst`, `removeLast`, etc.)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary

| Type         | Backing              | Prefer when                         |
| ------------ | -------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `ArrayList`  | Resizable array      | Fast index access / general use     |
| `LinkedList` | Doubly linked nodes  | Frequent end/start add-remove       |

🎤 Interview answer (say this):
`ArrayList` is a resizable array — `get(i)` is `O(1)`, but inserting/removing in the middle shifts elements (`O(n)`).
`LinkedList` is a doubly linked list — no shifting, but finding an index costs `O(n)`, so `add(index, e)` / `remove(index)` are still `O(n)`.
Prefer `ArrayList` for most cases (especially random access). Prefer `LinkedList` when you mainly add/remove at the ends or need `Deque` operations.

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) “LinkedList middle insert is always `O(1)`” — false for `list.add(i, x)` / `remove(i)`; traversal is `O(n)`. `O(1)`
only if you already have the node (e.g. via `ListIterator`).
2) Both are **not** thread-safe.
3) `LinkedList` implements `Deque` — good for queue/stack; `ArrayList` does not.
4) In real apps, `ArrayList` often wins even for inserts because of better cache locality — don’t pick `LinkedList` by default.
5) Declare as `List` (or `Deque` if you need deque APIs), create with the concrete class you need.
